% Folk songs from A. H. Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan (Oxford:
% Clarendon Press, 1914), chapters I and II, "A Musical Diary": songs he
% took down from singers on his journey of 1910-11, with his English
% singing translations. Public domain everywhere (the author died in 1948;
% US publication 1914). Scan: https://archive.org/details/musicofhindostan00foxs
% (scan page = printed page + 48).
%
% A first reading from the scan, 2026-11-09, to be checked against the plates
% at the pages given: the words are certain, the rhythms nearly so, and the
% pitches were read from the engraving at high magnification, so a note may
% be a step out where the N: line says check. These tunes are diatonic and
% carry no %%tuning line; Fox Strangways's "Tonic" note is given in N:.
% The Garhwali songs (pp. 50-1) are in 5/8, which he barred as written here.

X:1
T:O give me those pretty eyes
T:Garhwali song, a dialogue
C:traditional (Garhwal)
O:India, Garhwal (Himalaya)
R:song
S:Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan (1914), Ex. 97, p. 51
N:Tonic G (G Dorian with the one-flat signature). He and she sing alternate verses. The metre changes from 3/4 to 2/4 at "make". Check: bars 3 and 4.
M:3/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=100
K:Gdor
%%style drone
G2 Bc Bc | cc c2 BB |[M:2/4] A2 Bc | c2 cB | A2 BB | G2 G z |]
w: O give me those pret-ty eyes, And I'll make you the gift in re-turn of my own, dear.
N:2 (She): And if I give you my pretty eyes, Other lovers 'll laugh and 'll leave me alone, dear.
N:3 (He): Then O let me borrow them, And keep them safe till you need them again, dear.
N:4 (She): For six months you shall borrow them; Promise truly you'll give me them back then again, dear.
N:5 (He): What word is this of a bargaining! I will keep your eyes and your heart and your soul, dear.

