w: Must you go, my Chhai_-la, now, Leav-ing me a-lone, my_ dar_-ling! Tell me, Chhai-la_ how I shall_ live when you are_ gone?
N:2: Shall I live, my Chhaila, say, Like a water-mill, my darling! Standing all the day desolate upon a hill?

X:6
T:Gurkha sing-song
T:Gurkha song, danced, with drum
C:traditional (Gurkha, Nepal)
O:India, Dharmsala (2nd Goorkhas)
R:song (solo and chorus, danced)
S:Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan (1914), Ex. 129, p. 57
N:From a sing-song of the men of the 2nd Goorkhas after a hockey match: a solo answered by a chorus, a drum under it, and one or two men dancing in the middle with bent knees, bent elbows and upturned palms, sometimes crouching for a bar or two. The songs came too fast for any words to be taken down, so sing it on "la" or play it: the chorus holds the high G while the solo would climb to it, then answers the solo note for note. A grunted "Ha! Ha!" sometimes ended a chorus.
N:The drum is Fox Strangways's: one bar of 6/8 under each bar of the voice's 4/4 (three against two), right hand on 1, 3 and 4, left on 5 and 6; which hand had the high head is not printed, so rim and bass drum are ours, as are the tempo, the drone on C and the name "upper mordent" for his ornament. High for young voices: rehearse it a fifth lower, in F.
M:4/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=108
K:C
