W:3. The golden nails no longer move On the divided cradle swung;
W:The song is sung; my ship is borne Safe home, my babe, on the ocean of love.

X:8
T:Nirguna's lullaby
T:Maratha lullaby
C:traditional (Maharashtra)
O:India, Maharashtra
R:devotional lullaby
S:Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan (1914), Ex. 162, pp. 66-67
N:Sung to Fox Strangways by a Hindu lady in Calcutta. The title names Nirguna, the supreme being without attributes in Hindu thought, so it is labelled a devotional song: schools follow their own guidance on religious songs. No words are printed under the notes; hum it, sing it on "la" or play it. The book's prose translation is a tired mother's song at the cradle: celestial carpenters made the cradle, her hand is tired of pulling its string, her throat is dry with singing, and the baby is not yet asleep. The tune lives on C, D and E with B flat and A below, so the tonic and the drone are C. Four lines: the first is repeated, then the other three are repeated together. The mark over the E in lines 2 and 3 stands for the "squeeze", a pressed emphasis on the note. The tune lies low: sing it a fourth or fifth higher. Guessed: no tempo is printed (crotchet 80 is ours); in the last bar of lines 1 and 2 the turn D E D is read as a quarter-note triplet, as lines 3 and 4 have C D there, so every bar is whole; the second note of line 1 bar 2, of lines 2 and 3, and the middle note of the triplet in line 4 bar 2 are best readings.
M:3/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=80
K:F
