X:9
T:The ayah's song
T:Hindostani song from the steamer
C:traditional (North India), sung by an unnamed ayah
O:India
R:air
S:Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan (1914), Ex. 160, p. 66
N:One of the songs sung on board a steamer by an ayah, a children's nurse travelling with a family in colonial times, whose name the book does not give. Fox Strangways took the tune down from her singing; he notes that her pitch centre moved and that the songs were hard for him to piece together, and he admired how the slide gave a wonderful value to the close and half-close. No words, language or purpose were recorded: hum it, sing it on "la" or play it, and let the slide (the four small notes before the half-close) be smooth and unhurried. The tune lies on C and ends on C, so the drone is on C; it reaches A below middle C, so sing it a fourth or fifth higher. Guessed: the second note of bar 4 (D, possibly C) and the rests in bar 2, the first ending and the last bar are best readings.
M:3/4
L:1/16
Q:1/4=80
K:C
